# Line-ending policy.
#
# The repository stores text as LF and checks it out as LF. Both halves are
# declared here so neither depends on a contributor's `core.autocrlf`.
#
# Why LF in the working tree and not the Windows-native CRLF: every generator in
# scripts/ writes LF (`writeFileSync` with \n) — the doc graph, Appendix D,
# FEATURE-MAP, the preflight report, the Postgres schema, the v1-inherited
# mirror — and the doc tooling's canonical form is LF too
# (scripts/canonical-text.mjs). Against a CRLF checkout those files land smaller
# than the index's cached stat, so git reports them modified forever while
# `git diff` stays empty. Making the checkout form match what the tools write
# removes that mismatch by construction rather than papering over it. The two
# script types that genuinely need CRLF are pinned below.
#
# Background: .brain/rca/2026-08-16-doc-graph-line-ending-hash-drift.md and
# .brain/rca/2026-08-14-business-knowledge-export-hash-newline.md.

# Default: let git classify text vs binary; normalize text to LF in the
# repository and check it out as LF. Later lines override per attribute, so
# eol=lf holds for everything below unless a line restates it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Text — declared so the auto heuristic is never consulted for a format this
# repository actually carries.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.js        text
*.jsx       text
*.mjs       text
*.cjs       text
*.ts        text
*.tsx       text
*.json      text
*.jsonl     text
*.md        text
*.css       text
*.html      text
*.svg       text
*.sql       text
*.prisma    text
*.py        text
*.yml       text
*.yaml      text
*.toml      text
*.txt       text
*.example   text
.gitignore  text
.gitattributes text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pinned to CRLF — the on-disk form is load-bearing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# cmd.exe reads a batch file line by line while it executes it, so an LF-only
# script can mis-parse labels, `goto` and multi-line blocks. run.bat is the
# double-click entry point for the app; it must always materialize as CRLF.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# PowerShell parses LF-only scripts correctly in both 5.1 and 7, so this pin is
# for consistency rather than correctness: every .ps1 here is Windows-only
# operator tooling invoked through powershell.exe alongside run.bat, and it is
# the one place a contributor might reasonably expect Windows-native endings.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pinned to LF — CRLF breaks the shebang (`/usr/bin/env bash\r`).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Never converted — byte-exact artifacts git must not rewrite.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trust anchors. PEM is technically text, but a pinned CA is an exact artifact —
# scripts/supabase-tls.mjs fingerprints it — and git has no business touching
# its bytes.
certs/**        -text
config/certs/** -text
*.crt       -text
*.pem       -text
*.cer       -text
*.key       -text
*.der       binary
*.pfx       binary
*.p12       binary

